As shown in Figs.  6  and  7 , repeated thermomechanical processing does not lead to pronounced changes in the characteristic thermal transition temperatures of the investigated PLA/PHB blend. Overall, the values of Tg, Tm and ΔHₘ remain relatively stable with increasing number of processing cycles for all recycling routes, indicating good thermal stability of the blend during mechanical recycling. This suggests that repeated processing primarily affects molecular weight and melt-state degradation behavior, while the fundamental phase structure and thermal transition character of the PLA/PHB blend remain largely preserved. This behavior represents a clear advantage from a technological perspective, as it suggests that repeated processing does not significantly alter the thermal–mechanical performance window of the material. A similar conclusion was also reached in the study [ 24 ], where multiple thermomechanical processing of PLA/PHB blends was shown to have no significant effect on their characteristic thermal transitions and overall thermal properties. Figure 6 Dependence of glass transition temperature  T g \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
